<p>A powerful winter storm hit the US Northeast on Monday, bringing blizzard conditions, heavy snowfall, and strong winds from Maryland to Maine. Several areas saw more than a foot (about 30 cm) of snow, and officials issued widespread weather warnings that covered tens of millions of people. The storm quickly upended travel and daily routines. Airlines canceled and delayed thousands of flights, while some train service and road travel were shut down or limited in places. Power outages also spread across multiple states as wind and snow knocked down lines. By Monday evening, conditions started to ease in parts of the Northeast, but cleanup and travel headaches were expected to linger as crews worked to clear snow and restore service.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/e4P_IxxGzJpPRjJDAzUT2exaQQZxWTc8iC16Ll3NcRfItZSmTSD4GlSha3v8_fMDNfSjN2wuKEQnLuHpMr_xXjVdl_1vMyW36hMFAyTNnCzG6iDYKQrN2S_1ZMnlS-p3_ubEMB6ifGToHQkr5zyipRXbrlo7xlwsS8NSdOTZASWP3DJWhJcSRiGBznbS3732uLsft0W_etqxnMzz8KFaJQ0p6ZmaLf3Xmn9tG5Uw9lbjYB8Nw8EJkJhTyK_FWhkdKApPnZNzUAvV6mmNLg' alt='A pedestrian walks as snow falls during a winter storm in New York City, February 23, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Snowfall extremes: Rhode Island’s TF Green International Airport logged close to 38 inches of snow by Monday evening, leading to canceled scheduled arrivals and departures for the day.</li><li>Local impacts: New York City’s Central Park measured more than 15 inches of snow by Monday morning, with another 5–6 inches forecast before the system tapered later in the day, according to the National Weather Service’s Weather Prediction Center.</li><li>Wind threat: Forecasters warned of wind gusts of roughly 40–60 mph (64–100 kph) from Delaware through New York City to Boston—strong enough to trigger whiteouts and deep drifts.</li><li>School decisions: Philadelphia moved to online learning, while New York City canceled public school classes for Monday.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>New York City government: Mayor Zohran Mamdani urged residents to stay off the roads so emergency crews could clear streets. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1975322/blizzard-pummels-us-northeast-disturbing-flights-and-road-network'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>US National Weather Service: The Weather Prediction Center is tracking the chance of another storm that could bring more snow later this week. (<a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/millions-stuck-at-home-as-historic-blizzard-hits-nyc/'>Michael West</a>)</li><li>Airlines (United Airlines): United said it planned to ramp up operations on February 24, but warned conditions were still challenging. The airline also said it had canceled 1,600 flights through February 25. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/storm-paralyses-travel-forcing-more-than-8000-us-flight-disruptions/article70669736.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/11-000-flights-grounded-500-000-homes-left-without-power-as-snowstorm-hits-us-11128125#publisher=newsstand'>11,000 Flights Grounded, 500,000 Homes Left Without Power As Storm Hits US</a> - ndtv.com</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/02/23/snowstorm-in-northeast-us-leaves-40-million-people-under-weather-warnings_6750807_4.html'>Snowstorm in northeastern US leaves 40 million people under weather warnings</a> - lemonde.fr</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/storm-paralyses-travel-forcing-more-than-8000-us-flight-disruptions/article70669736.ece'>Storm paralyses travel, forcing more than 8,000 U.S. flight disruptions</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-winter-storm-schools-disrupts-travel-us-northeast/'>Powerful winter storm in U.S. Northeast closes schools and disrupts travel</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.firstpost.com/world/blizzard-dumps-over-a-foot-of-snow-across-us-northeast-halts-5700-flights-13982894.html'>Bliz